Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.000
Let's get started.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.500
So we are towards the
tail end of the course.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.410
I saw some of your project
proposals submissions,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.960
and it's looking good.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.275
Keep making up the progress.

00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:23.650
Today, we are
going to be talking

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.230
about the human angle
in lossy compression,

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.930
particularly focused on
the vision aspect of it.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.300
So it's another sort
of special topic,

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.180
and it's going to
be a fun lecture.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.220
It's kind of stuff,
which is typically not

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.230
covered in information
theory, but it's still

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.070
finds its ways into practical
lossy compressors and stuff.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:46.730
Yeah, any questions by
the way so far on learning

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.020
or logistics or anything?

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:52.010
Good.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:52.510
No?

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.090
OK.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:53.960
So yeah.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.580
So so far in lossy
compression, we

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.740
have looked at a lot of
different things, right?

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.810
So we have looked
at quantization,

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.900
which is a core mechanism
of introducing loss.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.320
We have looked at third
underpinning of rate distortion

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:06.860
trade-off.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.560
Homework 4 also has a
question to get you warmed

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.420
up and practiced about it.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.270
We have spent some time
looking at optimal solutions

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.780
in the case of Gaussian sources,
which builds up our intuitions,

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.410
as well as particularly focusing
on MSE distortion, right?

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.860
So far in the course, mostly
we have stuck with MSE.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.040
And then we talked about image
compression and JPEG and learnt

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.690
image compression.

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:33.700
OK?

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.510
So this class is going
to be very different.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:41.320
So the main point is that
all multimedia, at least

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.510
eventually is consumed
by humans, right?

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.270
So it's like it's for
the human consumption.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.860
And human sensory
like human senses

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.190
play a big role in the design
of these lossy compressors

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.660
for multimedia.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.850
And we are going to
understand like what

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.930
properties of this sort of
human sensory perception

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.290
comes out and plays in
our design of compressors.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.830
So in particular, we'll
look at questions like,

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:10.960
so why some of the
design decisions

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.730
were made in the
image compressors

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.640
we saw like JPEG in particular?

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.630
Or how can we further improve
image video compression knowing

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:23.740
the fact that, oh, this content
or this whatever, signal

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.670
is eventually supposed
to be consumed by us?

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:26.170
Right?

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.060
And not just by machines.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.130
Finally, I think we have touched
on a little bit about it,

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:35.080
but want to just give you
guys a flavor on like, OK, MSE

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.700
is not ever encompassing, right?

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.270
So what are some
other options which

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.515
you might see if you
practice compression outside?

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.300
OK.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.540
So this talk is derived from
lots of great resources.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.860
Feel free to check them out.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.840
So even before we
go into images,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.000
so this is really like--

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.250
so these are number one, OK?

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.960
So what I'm showing
here on the right

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.990
is using this tool media info.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.890
And this is a compress
Pokemon theme dot MP3 file.

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.070
What this media info does is
like it tells you various stuff

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.300
about this compressed files.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.870
So for example, file size,
duration, so on and so forth.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.850
Here, you see the
compression format

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.760
is like MPEG audio, which
is like parallel to JPEG.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.380
It's just omnipresent.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.810
And then if I keep going
down, at some point,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:29.010
I see two channels,
and I see sampling

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:34.800
rate of 44.1 kilohertz, right?

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.610
Why do you think like this file
or typical audio compressors

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.850
have two channels or this
particular sampling rate?

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:45.450
Yeah.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:45.950
Oh.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.330
I guess human hearing goes
like 20 kilohertz or so.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.580
So 44 would let you--

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.250
or accurately use
up to 22 megahertz.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.420
Yeah, so OK.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.060
As an answered, like
he said, human hearing

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.470
goes up to 20 kilohertz.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:00.730
That seems to give a hint.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:01.990
That's exactly correct.

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.600
So the two channels is
because we have two ears.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.452
If humans had three
ears, maybe this file

00:04:06.452 --> 00:04:08.410
would have been compressed
with three channels,

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.060
and that would have
made better sense.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.520
So this very practical
design decision, right?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.089
So this like if I keep
this file uncompressed,

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.820
you are keeping 2x the
number of channels.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.410
This very practical
design decision

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.399
was taken into account.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:22.390
Very simple fact.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:23.590
Like we have two channels.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.930
And sometimes, you want like
stereo sound in your stuff,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:27.430
right?

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.110
So this is why two channels.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.893
And 44.1 kilohertz is
exactly again what Sean said.

00:04:31.893 --> 00:04:33.310
So if you-- I don't
know-- go back

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.520
to your elementary
biology, like you

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.450
might have heard that oh
humans can hear from 20 hertz

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:40.720
to 20 kilohertz.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.750
Though in practice, I don't know
any human who could actually

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:45.620
hear 20 kilohertz.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:47.180
So if you can, that's great.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:51.350
And so if you know about
Nyquist criteria you would

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.750
you would need two times
the highest frequency

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.840
to represent the signal
without any aliasing.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.050
And that's where the
40 kilohertz come from.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:02.458
And then 44.1 is even more fun.

00:05:02.458 --> 00:05:04.250
So you want to give
some buffer because you

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.542
don't have perfect low pass
filters, which is typically

00:05:06.542 --> 00:05:08.330
required in these
sampling techniques.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.570
So you have to give
some buffer over 40.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:14.510
And then 44.1 in particular
is very interesting

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:18.140
because it can be factorized
by 2, I think even 3,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.270
and 5 and multiple
different primes.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.270
So that allows you to later
on, downsample the signal

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.550
by throwing away samples
much more easily.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.390
So even this innocuous,
very random looking number,

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:30.800
it didn't magically come out.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.560
People thought about it,
sat down, and then like,

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.850
this is now default.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.340
So almost every file, like
you don't think about it when

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.610
you are saving an MP3 file,
but these design decisions

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:42.770
are there, and they are
determining the file sizes,

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:43.270
right?

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.445
So I could have had double
the sampling rate, half

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.820
the sampling rate, and that
would have completely changed

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:52.150
my compression, and file sizes,
and the kind of reconstructions

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.560
we would have had.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:57.940
So very direct example of how
humans are playing a big role.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.070
Like just the idea that
humans are going to consume

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.820
this plays a big role.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:03.330
OK.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.590
Then teaser number 2.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.420
I have already
asked this question.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.700
Maybe let me ask it
again just, or I'll just

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:09.780
go through this fast.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.940
But this is really a
very iconic picture

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.310
because it very clearly
suggests what's happening

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.410
in the image compression case.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.780
So here, I have
some source image,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.080
and all this BCDF
images basically

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.620
have the same mean squared
error with respect to a.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.790
But clearly, if you look
visually, for me at least,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.790
b is much, much better
reconstruction than any

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:33.840
of the c, d, e, f.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.520
For example, c is like smooth,
and e is extremely smooth.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:42.900
d, e, and f have just clear
noise sprinkled over, right?

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.140
So again, this clearly
says that not only

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:53.560
that a lot of design decisions
were made with humans in mind.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.350
If you are not careful about
designing your compressors

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.870
and future, without
taking that into account,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.067
you could have designed
a compressor, which

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:02.650
would have resulted
in reconstructions

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.450
like this, which is just bad.

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:05.830
That's a bad design.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:07.720
Like nobody practically
would like to keep

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.780
a compressor, which generates
reconstructions like that.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.750
So it's also important
to understand really

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:18.700
for the design, practical
design of compressors.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:19.200
OK.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.060
So rest of the
lecture will just get

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.080
some preliminary understanding
of how human vision works.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.720
We're going to focus on the
visual aspect of things.

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.820
We're not going to
talk about the audio.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.550
We'll see its role in the design
of traditional compressors,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.688
learn a bit more about
perceptual metrics,

00:07:34.688 --> 00:07:36.480
and the last part would
be like, how do you

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.150
account for perceptual metrics
eventually in this rate

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.770
distortion kind of framework?

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:44.320
So let's get started.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:45.470
Again, it'll be fun.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:45.970
It's light.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:47.890
So it's going to be different.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.090
So do interact.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.340
Let's see how this goes.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:56.140
So this is just like a
typical human eye anatomy.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.320
So here, you have some
cornea pupil, your eyelids,

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:03.130
which basically focus
any optical signal

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:08.230
and to the back of your eye,
which is this retina tissue.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.678
Here at retina basically,
you have transduction,

00:08:11.678 --> 00:08:13.720
which happens, which
converts these light signals

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:14.920
into electricity.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.310
And finally, like
through this disk--

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:19.330
sorry not optic disk.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.530
Through your optic nerve.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.540
You send back these
electrical signals back

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.790
to the brain, which
then process and makes

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:26.960
you see different things.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.732
So right here is our
first thing like when

00:08:31.732 --> 00:08:33.440
you have a light
source, which is coming.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.650
Suppose you have a light source
which has a single intensity.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.590
So suppose you had a delta
function in terms of pixels,

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:40.250
right?

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.830
Everything is black zero,
and then just one pixel

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.840
having very high intensity.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:49.010
When you look at
back of the retina,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.480
it's no more a delta function.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.830
What happens is that this
line function spreads out, OK?

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.930
So you have a low
pass filtering,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.870
which your eye is doing right
at the beginning just because

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.710
of the physics and
optics of things

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:06.210
even before anything
has happened.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.750
So this is important
because this

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.090
kind of goes back and
correlates to our fact, right?

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:14.060
That you can throw back
high frequency stuff.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.780
That's because, hey, if there
were like many of these lines

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:19.280
close to each other,
I wouldn't have

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.410
been able to distinguish
them anyways.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.830
So there is no point of keeping
extremely high frequency stuff.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.950
And interestingly, like you
can argue like nature also

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:31.910
has signals, which have very
low power in the high frequency

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:33.050
spectrum, right?

00:09:33.050 --> 00:09:35.720
And you can always
argue like humans

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.030
were designed to pick
up signals like that

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:41.460
versus like this is just like
the basic optics of things,

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:41.960
right?

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.160
So it's interesting.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.290
Right there, you
have something which

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:47.150
you can utilize in
your compressors

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:51.470
as soon as you understand
this very basic thing.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.540
So this is a bit more detailed.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:54.890
Don't have to worry about it.

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.800
This is just like the blown
up part of this retina.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.370
What you need to basically
think about is this.

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:05.900
So suppose you're watching
your favorite Tom Cruise movie.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:09.740
What happens is the light kind
of falls in this first region,

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.450
which is your photoreceptors.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:14.090
So this is just like
blown up part of retina.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.480
These photoreceptors are what
transduce this visual signal

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:19.250
into electrical signals, OK?

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.730
So you have light to electrical
conversion like the wavelengths

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:23.930
to electrical conversion.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.150
Physical property
to the electricity.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:28.820
And then you have a
lot of these neurons,

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:32.000
which are called RGCs or
Retinal Ganglion Cells-- again,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:34.730
don't have to worry about
the names and details as long

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:37.010
as you follow
through the ideas--

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:39.240
which are basically
these spiking neurons.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.240
So these issue like
electrical spikes,

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.580
which are then sent
back to the brain, which

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:45.140
processes these
electrical spikes

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.690
and bring out some
visual picture for you

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:50.630
like whatever you are
seeing in this real world.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.300
And so here is the second
big difference sort of

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.490
compared to how
we think of images

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:57.590
versus how humans process it.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:02.658
So we kind of treat images
as an array of pixels, right?

00:11:02.658 --> 00:11:03.950
Like that's what you have seen.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.690
That's what you
probably just think.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:07.893
If I ask you generally
like sitting around,

00:11:07.893 --> 00:11:08.810
like what is an image?

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.670
You will probably say,
oh, three RGB values.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.130
And that's basically
an image, right?

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.590
But humans are not really
perceiving it like that.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:18.440
What happens in humans
is that you basically

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:21.860
have many different kinds of
these retinal ganglion cells.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:23.030
Around 20.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:26.390
And each of them are
basically independently tiling

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:28.700
the whole screen, whole scene.

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.400
So it's like think
of this as like one

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.020
set of retinal ganglion cells.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.460
Yellow ones as the other.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:35.660
Green one as the third.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.910
Blue as the fourth, and
so on, and so forth.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.190
And you can think
that each of them

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:43.820
basically sends an independent
signal to the visual cortex.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.500
So it's like you have--

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:47.300
and it's debatable
a lot of things,

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:49.280
like we don't know
very precisely.

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.080
But this is still a
relatively advanced field

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.040
of [AUDIO OUT]
cells are basically

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:57.770
extracting different
features of the visual scene

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.710
and then sending it back
to the brain, right?

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.040
So which is very different than
how you would think of all DC,

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:06.260
where each pixel is
basically doing one thing.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.750
And then you build
machine learning models

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.720
or whatever it is to
extract different features.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.840
And humans case like
it's like, you already

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:18.047
have these feature extractors
in some sense right

00:12:18.047 --> 00:12:19.880
at the beginning right
at the retina, right?

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:23.060
And then you, on top of it,
build using your visual cortex

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:27.830
like much more complicated
associations in your brain.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:31.770
So again, like the
differences-- human retina

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:32.910
is not your camera.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.050
It's not just pixel array.

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:36.120
It's doing something
very different.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:36.900
The back end.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:43.570
In particular I think
these rod and cone cells--

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:45.190
like a lot of this
is even [AUDIO OUT]

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:48.220
GK level you might have
heard at some point.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:50.260
But it's very
interesting because these

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:53.380
do a lot of interesting things.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:58.240
So these rod and cone cells are
really important because these

00:12:58.240 --> 00:12:59.380
are our photoreceptors.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.450
These are our
sensors this is how

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.730
we are sensing the physical
signal coming into our eye

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.160
and making some
interpretation out of it.

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:10.300
And these are actually
very, very amazing, OK?

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:15.280
So first of all rods they
are responsible for encoding

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:16.060
intensity.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.610
So their only role
is to figure out

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.560
like, what's the intensity
of the scene which

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.200
is coming in no color
information, nothing else?

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:26.020
And these are
amazing because they

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:27.610
are like quite
[AUDIO OUT] a number

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.650
like almost a 100 million.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:32.350
But even more than that
they are able to adapt

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:35.770
so as to see almost a dynamic
range of 10 raise to 9.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.290
Like almost a billion
orders of magnitude.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:40.760
What I mean by
that is like think.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:45.110
If you are a human, you can be
sitting and outside California

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:46.550
summer weather.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.740
After a while, you can
see everything there.

00:13:48.740 --> 00:13:50.330
And then suddenly,
you have a class.

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.790
You rush to your class,
and it's a dark room.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:54.937
OK, initially, you are
not able to see anything

00:13:54.937 --> 00:13:57.020
for maybe a few seconds,
but within a few seconds,

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:58.100
your eyes adjust.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:01.400
And you are able to see
everything in this room.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.550
Now, think about what's really
the physical signal which

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:05.660
is coming into your eye?

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:06.200
Right?

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.680
When you are outside in
the sun, your intensity

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.990
was extremely high, right?

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.000
And then everything
else which was happening

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.460
was around this base
level, whereas when

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.560
you came into this
dark room, suddenly,

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:20.690
the intensity fell
significantly, right?

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.240
And then you were basically
seeing stuff around this level,

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:24.740
OK?

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:27.440
And this is a hard problem
if you think about it, right?

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.280
Like our cameras have struggled
to adapt to it till date.

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:32.600
Like I don't know.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.670
Iphones-- one of the
selling point is great.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.410
Night pictures, right?

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:37.010
Like why?

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:38.990
Because it's hard
to detect things

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:41.000
at that level of
intensity level.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.890
And we, as humans,
we just do it,

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:44.720
and we don't even
realize, right?

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:45.800
When we are doing that.

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.930
And rods basically have--

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:49.460
they are the contributors.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:52.040
Their only role is to
adapt to intensity figure

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:56.540
out, and change sort of like
the base level around which they

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:59.330
are going to see
the fluctuations

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:03.200
and see things around you, OK?

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:08.000
And another very
interesting thing about rods

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:12.630
is so if you look at
this diagram here,

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:16.360
you can think of your eye
as like a spherical surface,

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:16.860
right?

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:18.410
And then there are--

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.920
you can put angles to the retina
of where things are happening.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.320
And very interestingly,
you have some optic nerve,

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.690
which is sending signals
back to the brain,

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:29.760
but where this
optic nerve connect?

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.110
You don't have any sensors.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.490
You don't have any retina.

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:35.310
So whatever faults and
sort of this region,

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:36.720
you can't detect it.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.240
And it's like a fun exercise
if you want to do that.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.590
I don't know.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:41.430
I find it fun.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:45.210
You just focus on your thumb,
and you keep seeing straight,

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:48.510
and keep moving your thumb.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:49.560
So I can see my thumb.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:50.490
I can see my thumb.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:51.360
I can see my thumb.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:52.260
I can see my thumb.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:56.220
It just vanishes for me,
and then it comes back.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.480
You can try it now.

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.500
Try it at home.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.440
That's like a consequence
of having this blind spot

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:04.440
basically that at
a certain angle,

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.300
I just have this
blind spot, where

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:08.310
I can't see because
all the light there is

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:11.330
being focused in this region.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:15.050
But even more interesting than
that-- so here is like y-axis

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:17.300
shows the density
of these receptors.

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.310
So you can think
of how many sensors

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:21.260
you have in that region.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:23.990
If you look at the
solid line, the rods,

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:27.830
so they are present almost
everywhere in the retina

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:31.280
except like this region, which
is your blind spot like around,

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.610
whatever, 20 degrees.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:38.990
The other cell type which are
called cones on the other hand,

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:40.820
if you see their density--

00:16:40.820 --> 00:16:44.780
cones are basically not present
almost in whole of retina

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:49.530
except near this almost
zero degree angle, OK?

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:53.520
And the role of cones is
exactly opposite to rods.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.380
So rods are just
basically trying

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:56.640
to understand the intensity.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.380
It's giving you
the dynamic range.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:01.410
Cones are responsible
for colors and details

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:03.570
and all those things, right?

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:05.609
And they are completely
absent in the retina

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:08.160
except for maybe a very small
part of your central vision.

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:12.319
Right?

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:16.020
And the central part is
called basically fovea.

00:17:16.020 --> 00:17:19.329
Again, why is this interesting?

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:22.170
So if you think
about it, if we only

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:25.170
have high acuity
or high resolution

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:28.500
vision in such a small part
like if you think about it--

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:29.910
this is just like a few angles.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.010
You can convert it
into physical angles.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:34.950
It's like really small region
of the physical world, which

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:40.530
you are sampling with sort
of this high fidelity.

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:44.310
Like other places, you are just
encoding like a grayscale kind

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:45.735
of level to get adjusted.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.750
So then how am I able to
see this sort of scene

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:54.010
in a very smooth
and nice fashion?

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:54.510
Right?

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:58.410
That's an interesting
question to maybe think about.

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:02.900
And the reason for
that is we are actually

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.900
never seeing any static scene.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.390
So when you think you
are like just looking

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.120
and you are seeing an image,
that's not really true.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:12.860
You are actually
not seeing an image.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:15.080
At any given point,
you are seeing a video,

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:17.720
and your eye is not really
focusing at one point.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:21.680
What it's doing is
your eye ball really--

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:22.860
it's not eyeball.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:23.360
Sorry.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:24.290
The eye in general.

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:26.540
Like it's doing these
movements called

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:29.660
saccadic movements,
which is just basically--

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.220
it's moving really fast.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:34.440
You don't even know, but
it's continuously doing this.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.110
The reason it's moving this
fast and it's doing this

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:40.110
is to basically keep
changing the point

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:41.670
of the physical
scene, which points

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:45.450
to my central region, my fovea.

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:46.440
So think of it.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:49.290
It's like there's a very small
point at the center, where

00:18:49.290 --> 00:18:52.080
you have high resolution.

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.870
Everything around it
is low resolution, OK?

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.580
So when I'm looking
at this scene,

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:58.320
if I just do this, if I just--

00:18:58.320 --> 00:18:58.980
I don't know.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:01.740
Right now, I'm just
completely focusing on clock.

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:03.960
And actually, all of your
faces all of this thing

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.250
has really vanished for me.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:08.430
I can't see it if I very
consciously focus, right?

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.710
But if I'm being subconscious,
I'm not consciously

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.830
putting my eye to say, OK,
just look at one point, what

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.915
my eye really is doing
it's sampling this region,

00:19:15.915 --> 00:19:17.040
but it's not staying there.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:20.130
It's like my microcircuits
are in like milliseconds case.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:22.560
It's basically just continuously
moving and sampling.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.260
And then your brain is
interpolating all this signal

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:32.340
to form this continuous kind of
smooth looking picture, right?

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:35.500
So again, like a
lot of fun facts.

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:38.510
This class is going to be
a lot of things like that.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.660
So yeah, so it's pretty cool.

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.480
Like when I first learned
about it, it was amazing.

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:46.060
Like my eyes are not
staying at the same spot.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:47.828
But now, you can
be like, Pulkit,

00:19:47.828 --> 00:19:49.120
why are you talking about this?

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:49.620
Right?

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:52.030
Like there are so
many GK questions.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.850
You can do so many
things this idea

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:59.710
that you have very high density
sensors in a single place.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:03.280
It's actually called foveation.

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:10.090
And it can be exploited in
some very interesting ways

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.400
in your design of
physical sensors

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:14.830
and how you do compression, OK?

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:17.950
So for example, what it
says is that at any given

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:22.480
point of time, I'm only sampling
a small region in high density.

00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:26.350
So then it makes me think,
maybe, we can exploit

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:27.850
this in some way, right?

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:31.930
Maybe, if I am able to track
human vision let's say,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:33.310
then I can.

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:34.030
If I know, right?

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:34.530
OK.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:36.700
At this particular second,
I'm just looking here.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.530
Let's say next second,
I'm looking here.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:41.560
And if I can work at this
latency and just track,

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.080
my human eye is going
to look more densely

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.150
at a particular
part of a scene when

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:48.700
you are watching your favorite
movie or anything it is.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:51.350
Then I don't need to give too
many bits to everything else,

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:51.850
right?

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:54.520
I can just give more
rate to wherever

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.720
my humans are focusing.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.510
And I can give zero
rate to things outside.

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:02.607
And this may sound very sci-fi,
but over the past few years,

00:21:02.607 --> 00:21:04.690
there have been like lots
of interest and research

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.510
projects trying to exploit
exactly this idea in the VR

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:08.230
setting.

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:10.540
So right now, we are only
focused on 2D setting,

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:12.700
but now, you can think
if you have a VR headset,

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:14.770
maybe we can do something
to actually track

00:21:14.770 --> 00:21:16.000
where humans are looking.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.400
And maybe, we can
figure out like how

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.470
to distribute bit rates
because in VR, you now

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:22.120
have a 3D environment, right?

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:23.710
You don't want to
give equal bits

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:25.360
to everything on the periphery.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:28.990
So the kind of things
which we are learning also

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.300
have implications for
future technology, right?

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.430
It's not just things
which we have right now.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:36.460
Like lots of cool projects
along this basic idea.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:44.520
Yeah, I think we talked
about the first one just

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:46.470
like [AUDIO OUT] if you
have seen this before.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:50.310
So the adaptation aspect of
human eyes-- it's basically

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:53.160
called Weber's Law, which
basically says that we only

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:58.920
care about relative changes, not
the absolute value of changes.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.230
Again, very small point
but it's very interesting

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.390
how you can exploit it
in your signal processing

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.520
and freshened aspects.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.650
What it says basically
is like a bit

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:12.780
change at a high value of
luminance value would not

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:15.120
really--

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:18.570
it won't be as significant
as a single bit change

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:22.300
at a low luminance value, right?

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.820
Even though it's
exactly one bit change.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.010
So you need to be
careful about when

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:28.815
assigning like rate
and all those things

00:22:28.815 --> 00:22:30.190
in these situations,
or maybe you

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:31.930
are uniformly
assigning the rate.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:34.510
In that case, you may
have different artifacts,

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:37.540
which might be visible to you
just because those artifacts

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:40.990
are occurring in
like low intensity

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:43.340
region versus the
high intensity region.

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:45.610
Yeah, again, this
aspect, maybe it

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:47.500
wasn't so important
20 years ago,

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:51.880
but we are in a world where
we have 4k TVS, HD, 4K.

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:53.980
Like I'm pretty sure
we are not going

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:55.900
to stop there if we
can fit all that data

00:22:55.900 --> 00:23:00.130
and be able to process as we
want like million more pixels

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:04.090
in our cameras, like million
more pixels in our TV, right?

00:23:04.090 --> 00:23:07.630
And at even 4K, HD level
like all of these effects

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.130
starts becoming
very significant.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:11.320
And like the big companies
spending big money trying

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:14.740
to understand these issues and
improve upon them because it's

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:16.300
like straightforward
money, right?

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:18.790
Each bit saved or
your user experience.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:23.210
So simple idea but can be
exploited in interesting ways

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:24.245
in engineering setting.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:29.220
OK.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:30.220
I'll pause for a second.

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:31.050
Any questions?

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:35.260
Nope.

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:37.180
All right.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:39.640
So the next thing
is-- so we were just

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:42.640
talking about cones,
which look at colors.

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:45.400
What this plot shows
is on the x-axis?

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:48.160
It's the wavelength
which is how light

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.770
is sort of colors are
represented physically, right?

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:53.230
Colors are just
different wavelengths.

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:56.680
So 400 to 700 nanometer is
roughly a visible range.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:58.690
350 to 700, I think.

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:02.440
And then y-axis is
basically the response

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:07.870
for these cones at different
levels of wavelength.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:09.460
So think of these as sensors.

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:11.650
These different sensors
respond differently

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.420
to different wavelengths, OK?

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.370
And then what you
see is that you

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.080
[AUDIO OUT],, which
basically respond

00:24:18.080 --> 00:24:20.390
to roughly like the-- it's
not really [AUDIO OUT]..

00:24:20.390 --> 00:24:23.150
It's something, but
it's roughly responding

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:26.570
to the average wavelength
in the visible spectrum,

00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:28.940
but then you actually
have three cones.

00:24:28.940 --> 00:24:32.420
And these three cones
are sometimes called LMS.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:34.910
This long, medium, small.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:36.650
Long for like long wavelength.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:38.060
Medium is for medium wavelength.

00:24:38.060 --> 00:24:39.350
Small is for small wavelength.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:44.795
So that's the [AUDIO OUT]
neuro terminology for these.

00:24:44.795 --> 00:24:47.730
Now, why do you think
[AUDIO OUT] three cones?

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:48.390
Right?

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:51.570
Do you think that makes sense?

00:24:51.570 --> 00:24:53.910
Is that something confusing?

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.880
The answer is on this
slide by the way.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.800
Don't even have to
think that much.

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:04.690
So I'm sure at some point,
you would have wondered,

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:05.650
oh, why RGB?

00:25:05.650 --> 00:25:07.960
Where did this RGB
thing came from?

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:10.120
At least, I used to
wonder quite a lot.

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:11.710
Like why RGB?

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.570
Why are we stuck with
these three colors?

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:17.080
And that actually comes
down because your physical,

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:20.590
like your human retinal
sensors cones actually come

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:21.610
in three flavors.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.370
And they are roughly sampling
the red color, the green color,

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:26.320
and the blue color.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:28.990
And that's why like
RGB is one of the ways

00:25:28.990 --> 00:25:31.330
you would like to
represent because back

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:35.920
in 1700s, a lot of philosophers,
where you couldn't actually

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:39.760
do these experiments through
other psychovisual experiments.

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:42.250
Figured out that,
OK, maybe RGB seems

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:47.710
to have some special
space place in our vision.

00:25:47.710 --> 00:25:50.980
And that basically later on, in
neuroscience, would figure out.

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:53.620
You could even figure out
like their exact response

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.840
characteristics, so
on, and so forth.

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.408
So again, like even
something like RGB has--

00:25:59.408 --> 00:26:00.700
it's actually other way around.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:02.893
Like RGB doesn't come from here.

00:26:02.893 --> 00:26:04.310
Since we have these
things, that's

00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:07.250
why RGB has found like
a special kind of place

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:10.415
in our thinking
about visual images.

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:17.070
And so this color model--

00:26:17.070 --> 00:26:20.010
this is also sometimes
called trichromatic theory

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:20.910
of color vision.

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:27.340
So fancy name but just
English trichromatic theory.

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:30.760
But interestingly,
trichromatic theory

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:35.110
is not the only theory
of colors, right?

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:37.210
Some point, you might have--

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:40.180
so these are two physical
colors, which are exactly same.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:42.460
I could have
represented this as RGB.

00:26:42.460 --> 00:26:44.260
Like some different
values of RGB,

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:46.690
which gives me a
certain color, or maybe

00:26:46.690 --> 00:26:49.690
you have a color
space called CMYK.

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:50.470
Probably not.

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:53.050
So this used to be used
in old school printers

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:54.700
with toners, where--

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:57.220
so RGB is like an
additive color space

00:26:57.220 --> 00:26:59.950
where, oh, I add that,
I add G, I get a color.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:03.010
In this case, like these
values of RGB gives this color,

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:06.520
but I can also think of it
as like CMYK, which are sort

00:27:06.520 --> 00:27:07.780
of like subtractive colors.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:10.600
So when you add CM and
Y, you get like a black.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:14.020
So it's like removing sort of
thing, but it's the same color.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:16.360
So nobody said like,
oh, I need to represent

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.560
this as just this RGB.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:20.860
I could have represented
this as CMYK.

00:27:20.860 --> 00:27:22.510
And actually, on D, right?

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:25.210
What I'm showing
is this is actually

00:27:25.210 --> 00:27:29.150
a very complicated and not
so obvious and confusing

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:30.350
topic for engineers.

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:32.720
Like where does these
things come from?

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:35.600
But actually, there have
been a lot and a lot

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:38.870
and a lot of studies on like
how colors are represented,

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:41.150
not just at the neuro
or engineering level,

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:42.955
but actually, at the
psychovisual level.

00:27:42.955 --> 00:27:44.330
So at the end of
the day, this is

00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:46.970
like a psychophysics
experiment, right?

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:48.440
Like what colors do you see?

00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:51.080
How can I represent
the same color?

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.600
And there have been
tons and tons and tons

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:55.910
like philosophers all
the way back, I think,

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.130
and starting from 1700s.

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.500
People have been
asking these questions

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.320
trying to understand them in
deeper and deeper details.

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:05.780
And this is something
which you might

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:08.930
hear if you work in image
compression, video compression

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:09.860
at some point.

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:14.060
So the CIE-- it's
like just a society--

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:17.840
In 1931, came up with something
called chromaticity diagram.

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:19.790
So again, chroma for color.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:27.250
And it basically looks like
2D space to represent colors.

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:30.850
And what you have is
like different standards

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:33.550
and companies, et cetera, have
adopted very different color

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:34.450
spaces.

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:37.750
So you have RGB,
which we know of.

00:28:37.750 --> 00:28:39.290
There is something called SRG.

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:39.790
OK.

00:28:39.790 --> 00:28:41.248
So first of all,
there is something

00:28:41.248 --> 00:28:42.370
called visible spectrum.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:45.640
Like so invisibly, we can
only see these colors.

00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:47.320
So this has a weird space.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:49.060
I'm not going to go
into details of why,

00:28:49.060 --> 00:28:53.060
but if you are interested,
come talk to me later.

00:28:53.060 --> 00:28:55.720
But yeah, so the
yellow one is the set

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.700
of colors, which your
human eye can see, OK?

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:02.310
And then in practice,
you actually

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.100
can't represent those many
colors in the same way

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:06.360
as you would like to.

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:09.390
So historically, people
came up with RGB,

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:13.740
then there is SRGB, which
is this black curve here.

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:17.670
Adobe had an RGB, which is like
Adobe RGB, which is this one.

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:21.890
So Adobe basically came up
with its own RGB standard.

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.630
Now, you should
ask like, OK, why

00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:25.160
am I talking about
these standards?

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:26.450
Right?

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:31.580
The reason is there's
224, 102, 102, right?

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:33.500
We simplified our
discussion of images,

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:36.470
and we just said, oh, it's
like 3 bytes per pixel,

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:38.630
which responds to one color.

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:39.830
But that's not true.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:41.870
At the end of the day,
these digital values

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:44.570
are converted into physical
signals using your monitor.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:47.600
Maybe, they are printed
using your printer.

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:51.830
Maybe the actual-- your
camera, when it was recording,

00:29:51.830 --> 00:29:55.280
it was converting this physical
signal to this RGB values.

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:57.410
And all of this sort
of depends on what's

00:29:57.410 --> 00:29:58.508
the color space, right?

00:29:58.508 --> 00:30:00.800
Like so for example, if a
display has a different color

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:03.470
space, then whatever
it was recorded with,

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:07.220
you will get very
different reconstruction.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:09.350
And that's important
for us because that

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:11.480
changes the distortion
completely, right?

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.880
So you might have to
think about these things.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:17.600
And again, it might seem like,
hey, we never think about it.

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:21.770
Do it, but it's
actually very common.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:23.427
And you have some
very interesting bugs

00:30:23.427 --> 00:30:25.760
which come up if you were
doing video compression, image

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:28.230
compression, if you are not
being careful about these very

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:31.470
small things because like
just different data recorded

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:32.250
in different way.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:33.450
Displayed in different way.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:38.032
And you'll be thinking, why is
my Huffman encoder not working

00:30:38.032 --> 00:30:38.740
or whatever else?

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:40.240
The variant not
working, but it will

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:43.280
be this color space issue,
which you might be sitting with.

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.720
So it's just important to
know that these things exist

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:46.890
and do play a role.

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:50.230
OK.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:52.850
So this is going to be fun.

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:55.300
So let's talk a little bit more.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:56.260
Let's do this.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:01.600
By the way, yeah, actually,
just a quick show of hands.

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:04.450
This dress I think blew up
sometime last year or the year

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:05.770
before.

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:09.145
How many of you see this
as a gold and white?

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:12.950
Oh.

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:14.150
OK.

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:15.260
What do you see it as?

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:15.890
Let me ask.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:17.000
I forgot what's--

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:17.630
Blue and black.

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:18.420
Blue and black.

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:18.920
Excellent.

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:19.420
So OK.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:22.220
How many of you see
it as blue and black?

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:23.060
Why is it happening?

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:26.810
It's the same RGB values
I'm showing here, right?

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:27.710
So right there.

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:30.560
It's very interesting,
and we'll motivate

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:32.480
this for the next thing.

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:37.700
But even before that, let's do
this quick experiment, I think.

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:38.440
Oh.

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:39.420
Yeah, it loaded.

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:39.920
OK.

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:41.720
So what I want you
guys to do is--

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:43.400
OK, first of all,
what do you see here?

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:44.420
Don't focus anything.

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:45.290
Just look around.

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:47.170
What do you see?

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:49.730
Anyone?

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.333
Well, see something.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:55.670
OK.

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.740
Do you see a vanishing
purple circle sort of, right?

00:31:58.740 --> 00:31:59.240
OK.

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:00.590
Now, focus on the x.

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:01.460
Just on the x.

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:03.005
I just want you
to focus on the x.

00:32:05.608 --> 00:32:07.650
And if this is going to
work in this room or not,

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:09.375
let me also come here.

00:32:09.375 --> 00:32:10.125
Oh, what happened?

00:32:13.970 --> 00:32:15.570
You see a yellow dot?

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:16.070
OK.

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:19.250
And anyone else?

00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:22.260
For a second, I saw a whole
circle of yellow dots.

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:24.510
You saw a couple of yellow dots?

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:25.350
It's interesting.

00:32:25.350 --> 00:32:28.335
I would have called that color
green, but maybe it's yellow.

00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:31.650
[AUDIO OUT]

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:33.450
Yeah, it's like
greenish yellow, right?

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.810
So if you keep focusing on
that x, the vanishing one,

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:41.420
suddenly, becomes like
a greenish yellow color.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:43.340
Like again, it's
not your vision.

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:44.840
It's nothing wrong
with your eyes.

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:50.300
You don't have to go get
new glasses after this.

00:32:50.300 --> 00:32:51.620
It's a known effect.

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:53.510
And that happens because--

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:56.630
so we talked about the
trichromatic theory of color,

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:00.920
but there is another theory
of color, which is basically

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:05.600
called this opponent process
theory of color vision, OK?

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:08.300
Which basically said that
there are pairs of colors which

00:33:08.300 --> 00:33:09.743
are negatives of each other.

00:33:09.743 --> 00:33:12.410
So the effect, by the way, which
I showed you in the last slide,

00:33:12.410 --> 00:33:14.960
it's called the
after images effect,

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:17.330
which is like if you
focus on one color,

00:33:17.330 --> 00:33:20.300
it's like your eyes are adapting
to subtract that color out.

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:23.570
So suddenly, when you
look at somewhere else--

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:24.080
oh, sorry.

00:33:24.080 --> 00:33:26.480
Your mean color is
somewhat very different.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:30.440
And so that white space in that
previous lilac chaser example

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:34.220
suddenly becomes like negative
of red, which is what you saw.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:37.250
Like not really negative
of red, but which

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:39.470
is that yellow green
whatever color you guys want

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:41.400
to call it, right?

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:45.540
So that's like again,
a very basic aspect

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:47.310
of how our human vision works.

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:53.630
And that's the sort of
origin of this YUV thing,

00:33:53.630 --> 00:33:56.888
which we have talked about
in the class before, right?

00:33:56.888 --> 00:33:59.180
Like we had this random color
transform, which we said,

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:00.260
OK, let's do YUV.

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:02.090
We are not going
to work with RGB.

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:05.180
And I said, OK, later on, we'll
talk about a little bit more

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:06.110
about this.

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:14.460
And it's basically this
theory of color vision, which

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.949
suggests the idea of this
opponent colors, which

00:34:16.949 --> 00:34:19.889
basically says that,
oh, maybe, we have

00:34:19.889 --> 00:34:22.590
this blue, green and red cones.

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:26.040
The way humans perceive it is
actually not these signals,

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:27.540
but you merge them.

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:29.070
You merge them in
a sense, where you

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:32.400
get this white and black signal,
which is basically somewhat

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:34.770
summation and addition of
all these three wavelengths

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:37.560
by this presence of all these
three black is subtraction.

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:39.449
Then you have a blue yellow.

00:34:39.449 --> 00:34:44.000
And then you have a red
green opponent colors.

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:47.330
And it's debatable.

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:50.330
There have been lots lots and
lots of empirical evidence

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.219
on all sorts of access.

00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:55.040
We haven't really finalized
on what is correct,

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:57.650
but it's like even
very recently,

00:34:57.650 --> 00:34:59.122
I think 2019 or [AUDIO OUT].

00:34:59.122 --> 00:35:01.580
There have been papers that
say, hey, this theory is wrong,

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:02.288
or this is right.

00:35:02.288 --> 00:35:04.220
Like there is more evidence.

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:06.740
And this has been going
on since I think 1800s.

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:08.750
So it's very interesting.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:12.230
But for better or worse,
we are stuck with this.

00:35:12.230 --> 00:35:14.930
And we have this shows up in
our engineering applications

00:35:14.930 --> 00:35:17.320
all the time.

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:21.270
So again, I just recall our
image JPEG compression, right?

00:35:21.270 --> 00:35:22.800
So we started with RGB.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:25.860
And the first step was
[AUDIO OUT] transformation.

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:27.690
And then we also
briefly touched upon

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:31.410
that we are going to downsample
the CBCR components or UV

00:35:31.410 --> 00:35:33.240
components.

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:38.720
And then [AUDIO OUT]
standard thing.

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:40.430
Now, we can understand
this better.

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:43.670
So why CBCR or UV?

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:46.408
Like all of them are
consider them equivalent.

00:35:46.408 --> 00:35:48.200
Like they have some
historical differences.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:51.770
But for today's class,
assume by YUV, YCBCR.

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:54.680
Sometimes, you'll see
star marks on top of it.

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:56.280
Like literally
like this CD star.

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:56.780
CR.

00:35:56.780 --> 00:35:57.170
Oh, yeah.

00:35:57.170 --> 00:35:57.440
Here.

00:35:57.440 --> 00:35:57.940
Sorry.

00:35:57.940 --> 00:35:58.880
Y-UV.

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:02.540
Sometimes, you will
see Y-UV, Y-CBCR.

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:04.490
Consider all of
them equal for now.

00:36:04.490 --> 00:36:08.330
There are slight nuances and
differences, which we can talk.

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:11.960
But the main reason for
maybe, if we think about CBCR,

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.070
it's very obvious.

00:36:13.070 --> 00:36:16.400
Why going for the
Luma component.

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:18.560
CB is going for
this blue yellow,

00:36:18.560 --> 00:36:21.740
and CR is sort of going
for this red green, right?

00:36:21.740 --> 00:36:24.823
So that's kind of
modeling the human vision.

00:36:24.823 --> 00:36:26.240
And that's why we
are splitting it

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:27.698
in these three
independent channels

00:36:27.698 --> 00:36:31.130
because that's what we think
our eye sort of works with.

00:36:31.130 --> 00:36:33.410
And so again, if
you have this image,

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:36.590
this is how your YCB
and CR might look like.

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:39.595
And we said that these still
have a lot of correlation,

00:36:39.595 --> 00:36:40.095
right?

00:36:44.130 --> 00:36:45.000
So OK.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:49.650
So this explains why we did the
color transformation from RGB

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:50.850
to YCBCR.

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:53.590
But if you recall, we also
talked about downsampling,

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:54.090
right?

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:55.770
And then I said
something that, oh,

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:58.500
like you actually downsample
the colored components more

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:00.510
than the luma ones
because we care

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:03.720
more about the average intensity
than the everything else.

00:37:03.720 --> 00:37:08.670
So that's the other reason
for actually even converting

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:10.993
your RGB signal into these
three independent channels

00:37:10.993 --> 00:37:12.660
because these three
independent channels

00:37:12.660 --> 00:37:14.070
are behaving differently.

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:16.500
You care more about Y than CBCR.

00:37:16.500 --> 00:37:20.970
And that again, comes from
human [AUDIO OUT],, OK?

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:23.940
So really the two reasons for
doing this color transformation

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:26.550
is one is like the
perceptual color space

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:30.000
based on this idea
of opponent theory

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:32.700
like that lilac chaser
or that dress thing.

00:37:32.700 --> 00:37:36.360
And the second main idea is
that you have different contrast

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:40.830
sensitivity for your color
channels versus luma channels.

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.270
If you haven't
heard this before,

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:44.430
let me just explain
the idea very quickly.

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:46.273
What your contrast
sensitivity say

00:37:46.273 --> 00:37:47.940
is this is like similar
to saying humans

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:50.100
can hear 20 hertz
to 20 kilohertz.

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:55.920
Similarly, there are
certain spatial frequencies

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:57.690
and certain contrasts.

00:37:57.690 --> 00:38:02.220
So you can think of contrast
just as the variance

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:03.030
around that point.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:06.990
Like how much the
level shifts when

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:09.420
you change 1 byte versus
2 byte versus 4 bytes.

00:38:09.420 --> 00:38:11.700
You are changing different
levels of intensity.

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:17.240
And so your contrast
really is like a function

00:38:17.240 --> 00:38:19.130
of your contrast plus
spatial frequency.

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:21.710
So what I have
here in this image

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:24.410
is there are bars with
increasing and increasing

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:27.420
spatial frequency, OK?

00:38:27.420 --> 00:38:30.810
And as you go down, I
have higher differentials

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:31.980
between these bars.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:34.270
Binary bars.

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:38.110
For me, I can make out like
bars maybe in this region.

00:38:38.110 --> 00:38:41.780
This red line is supposed to
be for a typical human, average

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:42.280
human.

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:43.720
Everything within
it-- you should

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:48.220
be able to distinguish as like
some lines versus around here.

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:50.528
You should sort of
see it as a gray area.

00:38:50.528 --> 00:38:52.570
Now, for different people,
it might be different.

00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:54.790
For example for me,
I actually think

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:56.290
can't see any of this region.

00:38:56.290 --> 00:38:57.700
Can't see this region.

00:38:57.700 --> 00:38:59.950
So my eyes are not
perfect, right?

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:05.790
So like you have this
different contrast sensitivity.

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:08.850
And this is important
because again,

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:12.480
what this tells is like whether
you can distinguish two signals

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.240
or not occurring at different
spatial frequencies.

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:18.010
Why do you care about this?

00:39:18.010 --> 00:39:19.510
Can somebody think
about why would I

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:24.220
care about this for
designing compressors?

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:30.570
Anyone?

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:33.975
Not that hard again.

00:39:33.975 --> 00:39:34.475
Yeah.

00:39:34.475 --> 00:39:36.590
Like, where the things are
like you can't see anyways.

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:38.150
I guess, you can
just compress them.

00:39:38.150 --> 00:39:38.740
Exactly.

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:41.240
Where the things I can't see
anyways, I can just throw away.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:44.180
So if I have some physical
signal or some RGB values

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:47.420
with luma values lying in here,
very low spatial frequency,

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:49.640
oh, I could have actually
increased the distortion.

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:51.890
Sure, my mean square
error would have been high

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:54.380
for that particular
reason, but I don't care.

00:39:54.380 --> 00:39:55.490
Like humans don't care.

00:39:55.490 --> 00:40:00.650
So again, very simple
idea, but immediate impact.

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:02.810
On the right,
basically is a plot

00:40:02.810 --> 00:40:05.750
which shows this exact
contrast sensitivity

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:07.070
against spatial frequency.

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:08.900
But for the
luminance component--

00:40:08.900 --> 00:40:12.950
so the Y sort of component
and the CBCR components.

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:15.470
And what you see is that
your contrast sensitivity

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:18.410
falls very fast for
the colored components.

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:21.420
And that again says is that,
oh, I can throw away more stuff.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:23.510
I don't really need
high resolution even.

00:40:23.510 --> 00:40:25.700
Like we talked about
throwing away bits.

00:40:25.700 --> 00:40:27.500
Maybe, I can reduce
the resolution.

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:30.020
I don't need-- why
do we need resolution

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:32.960
to keep account of higher
and higher spatial frequency?

00:40:32.960 --> 00:40:35.630
What this suggests is you
can throw away colored.

00:40:35.630 --> 00:40:37.670
You can downsample
the colored signals.

00:40:37.670 --> 00:40:39.680
And that's exactly
what we do, right?

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:43.250
So the downsampling step is sort
of applied to the CBCR channels

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:44.630
and not to the Y channels.

00:40:44.630 --> 00:40:48.560
And things still work out
fine, which again, is motivated

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:51.750
by how your human vision works.

00:40:51.750 --> 00:40:53.540
So this particular
curve actually

00:40:53.540 --> 00:40:56.930
has lots of implications for us.

00:40:56.930 --> 00:40:59.705
So again, like the higher
quantization of high frequency.

00:40:59.705 --> 00:41:02.450
DCT components, you can
again trace it back.

00:41:02.450 --> 00:41:03.560
I have been handwaving.

00:41:03.560 --> 00:41:05.120
Humans don't see high frequency.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:06.680
What does that really mean?

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:09.440
Like this is one way
to quantitatively think

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:11.060
about what we mean
by high frequency

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:14.060
and what we can't
see really, right?

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:16.940
This has implications
for chroma subsampling--

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:18.200
what we just talked about.

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:20.300
Why we do chroma subsampling?

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:23.330
Something which we didn't talk
about in class in as detail

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:25.970
but just like Han verbally
mentioned, you actually

00:41:25.970 --> 00:41:28.700
have different quantization
matrices itself

00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:31.410
for luma and chroma components.

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:34.140
So that basically says you
have a very different design

00:41:34.140 --> 00:41:37.740
of rate distortion trade-off
for the luma components

00:41:37.740 --> 00:41:39.540
versus the chroma components.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:43.120
And that again, is motivated
by just this curve.

00:41:43.120 --> 00:41:45.420
And all of these are
used in JPEG, right?

00:41:45.420 --> 00:41:47.790
Like people knew about it.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:51.587
This is being exploited in JPEG.

00:41:51.587 --> 00:41:53.420
I think I want to show
you this thing first.

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:55.910
So this is just
an example, right?

00:41:55.910 --> 00:41:57.960
So we looked at it in
the previous class.

00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:00.320
So by CBCR, you take this RGB.

00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:01.430
You convert YCBCR.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:03.500
You downsample the CBCR.

00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:06.102
I just wanted to remind the
numbers, and there is a demo.

00:42:06.102 --> 00:42:08.060
You can go in that link
and play with this idea

00:42:08.060 --> 00:42:12.140
of how much you can subsample
without losing any perception.

00:42:12.140 --> 00:42:15.360
When you don't downsample, this
is what the image looks like.

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:18.860
And when you do JPEG
compression on top of it,

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:23.900
you get like three 23 kilo
bit from 429 kilobits.

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:26.690
All of these images are
roughly at think of them

00:42:26.690 --> 00:42:30.840
at similar level
of distortion, OK?

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:35.220
Versus if I do this chroma
downsampling, and then

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:36.000
I-- so OK.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:39.240
Chroma downsampling itself gave
me some obviously advantage

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:40.350
in terms of compression.

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:44.520
So I got like 429 to
352, but personally, I

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.160
can't see any difference
between these two images, which

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:48.060
is the whole point.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:50.040
Like we as humans,
our eyes work that way

00:42:50.040 --> 00:42:51.900
that I can't see
these differences.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.610
So that's advantage number one.

00:42:53.610 --> 00:42:55.950
Just downsampling gave
me some advantage.

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:58.440
But then since I
have downsampled.

00:42:58.440 --> 00:43:00.480
Now recall how
JPEG works, right?

00:43:00.480 --> 00:43:02.130
Since I have
downsampled, now there

00:43:02.130 --> 00:43:04.200
is higher correlation
between neighboring pixels

00:43:04.200 --> 00:43:05.430
for these channels.

00:43:05.430 --> 00:43:09.810
So my compressor on top of
this straight-up advantage

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.720
by downsampling can also exploit
that there is more correlation

00:43:12.720 --> 00:43:14.010
in this channels.

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:16.500
And so once you do
this downsampling

00:43:16.500 --> 00:43:19.680
and then you apply JPEG, you
get like almost similar quality

00:43:19.680 --> 00:43:22.290
image at 176 kilobit.

00:43:22.290 --> 00:43:23.000
Kilo bit.

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:25.800
Yeah, which is like
almost half of if you

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:27.300
weren't applying
this downsampling

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:29.100
and then using JPEG.

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:31.230
So the advantage is
sort of multiply.

00:43:31.230 --> 00:43:33.870
It's not just staying there.

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:36.780
And the way this
down sampling works

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:40.620
is like you will hear terms
like 4:4:4, 4:2:2, 4:2:0.

00:43:40.620 --> 00:43:43.950
So for example here,
it's called YUV 4:2:0.

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:47.970
You will hear terms
like this quite often.

00:43:47.970 --> 00:43:50.430
What this 4:2:0
sort of is saying

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:53.190
is just saying it's defining
how are you down sampling

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:54.750
the chroma components.

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:58.620
So for example, 4:4:$
is like full resolution.

00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:01.230
So if you had eight colors,
you are representing it

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:03.990
as some eight blocks of
colors or eight pixels,

00:44:03.990 --> 00:44:06.750
whichever way you
want to think of it.

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:09.840
You have some y components,
and you have CRC components.

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:13.140
And you have unique values for
each of these pixels, right?

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:15.810
But when you are down sampling,
what, for example happens,

00:44:15.810 --> 00:44:19.440
in 4:2:0 is obviously, you keep
the same y, whatever y you had.

00:44:19.440 --> 00:44:25.020
But now, you take the average
of the UV components basically.

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:28.080
So now, instead of using
eight colors to represent it,

00:44:28.080 --> 00:44:31.530
you are only using four colors.

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:35.020
So you combine this to blocks,
these 2 blocks, these two.

00:44:35.020 --> 00:44:35.520
Sorry.

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:36.540
Two colors, not four.

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:37.140
My bad.

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:39.732
So you are basically combining
this into a single block.

00:44:39.732 --> 00:44:41.940
So you are throwing away a
lot of information, right?

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:44.610
From this color to just
went to a single color.

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:49.500
And 4:2:0 is like default
option and a lot of like video

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:50.925
compressors, image compressors.

00:44:50.925 --> 00:44:52.050
It's actually quite common.

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:56.070
So here, like for
example, it's worth 4:2:0.

00:44:56.070 --> 00:45:01.995
And yeah, so it's good
that we have this property

00:45:01.995 --> 00:45:03.370
but we have to be
careful, right?

00:45:03.370 --> 00:45:05.320
So this is something
which we introduced.

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:08.230
And this now leads to some
very interesting issues

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.040
in life and practice.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:13.450
So now, suppose you had a very
high frequency like you just

00:45:13.450 --> 00:45:16.420
had an array of pixels,
where the 4 pixels were red

00:45:16.420 --> 00:45:18.610
and everything
else was blue, OK?

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:22.150
Now, if you downsample it
to something like 4:2:0,

00:45:22.150 --> 00:45:25.280
you actually get an image
which looks like this.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:27.820
And in this case, now,
there are very specific

00:45:27.820 --> 00:45:29.590
chromatic artifacts, right?

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:33.040
It's like you will
see different color.

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:35.350
So where do you see
these effects even more

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:36.550
like these edge effects?

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:39.610
So for example let's imagine
you had a terminal screen grab.

00:45:39.610 --> 00:45:43.030
Like "The quick brown fox
jumps over the lazy dog."

00:45:43.030 --> 00:45:44.680
This is like your screenshot.

00:45:44.680 --> 00:45:46.540
And let's say I'm just
using my compressor.

00:45:46.540 --> 00:45:47.710
I applied my compressor.

00:45:47.710 --> 00:45:50.080
It does YUV 4:2:0 down sampling.

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:51.860
In that case, what will happen?

00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:54.160
It's going to do this
sort of averaging

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:55.990
of pixels in 2:2 window.

00:45:55.990 --> 00:45:57.820
And this is the
output which you get.

00:45:57.820 --> 00:46:00.770
So in this case, you still
see maybe the right edges,

00:46:00.770 --> 00:46:02.720
but the color itself
changed, right?

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:05.420
So this is suddenly
like in terms

00:46:05.420 --> 00:46:08.180
of maybe mean square error,
not that high difference

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:11.450
compared to just applying
YUV 4:2:0 in some other case,

00:46:11.450 --> 00:46:13.250
but this is extremely lossy.

00:46:13.250 --> 00:46:15.420
You just change
the color itself.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:17.180
So you have to be
careful while doing

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:18.590
these down sampling and things.

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:20.630
Like these artifacts
show up in different ways

00:46:20.630 --> 00:46:21.870
in different situations.

00:46:26.800 --> 00:46:27.300
OK.

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:28.633
I'm going to pause for a second.

00:46:28.633 --> 00:46:29.760
Any questions?

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:47.590
OK.

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:48.910
Final topic.

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:54.040
Final, I guess, point
about YUV is cool.

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:55.480
We talked so much about YUV.

00:46:55.480 --> 00:46:56.260
Great.

00:46:56.260 --> 00:46:56.860
Right?

00:46:56.860 --> 00:46:59.650
But what exactly is the
this RGB to YUV conversion?

00:46:59.650 --> 00:47:03.700
Like how do you go from
RGB values to YUB values?

00:47:03.700 --> 00:47:05.500
Again, if you're
a complete newbie,

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:07.660
you probably
wouldn't care, right?

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:10.150
But this is again,
I would say 101

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:11.920
in video and image compression.

00:47:11.920 --> 00:47:13.300
Like if you are
working on these,

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:16.570
these are the issues you
struggle with many times

00:47:16.570 --> 00:47:18.790
in practice in life.

00:47:18.790 --> 00:47:23.320
And the answer really
is it's not as simple

00:47:23.320 --> 00:47:27.700
as it sounds because of
all that color spaces mess,

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:28.720
which we talked about.

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:31.450
All the color spaces can
mean many different things.

00:47:31.450 --> 00:47:33.550
And so there are various
different standards.

00:47:33.550 --> 00:47:36.910
For example, for your
SDTV color space,

00:47:36.910 --> 00:47:39.130
there is one standard
to convert your RGB.

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:41.620
So this is just like a
matrix multiplication, right?

00:47:41.620 --> 00:47:46.480
And similarly, for your
SRGB color space with HDTV,

00:47:46.480 --> 00:47:50.930
you have some other
YUV conversion matrix.

00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:53.570
And many times, you again
get very interesting box

00:47:53.570 --> 00:47:55.940
if you are like practically
working on these things,

00:47:55.940 --> 00:47:58.550
if you are not being careful
about the color space, which

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:01.798
you are working with like
the conversion color space.

