00:50:21.950 --> 00:50:26.000
decisions in JPEG, which is
very famous traditional image

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:27.710
compressor.

00:50:27.710 --> 00:50:31.490
Now, let's talk about this idea
of distortion metric, right?

00:50:31.490 --> 00:50:34.970
So thing we have said at
least 10 times this points

00:50:34.970 --> 00:50:37.880
in this class that FC
is a bad distortion

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:43.010
metric for practical
components, and our golden image

00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:44.330
is back to help us.

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:52.030
So roughly, so like the idea is
to go towards more perceptual

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:52.570
metrics.

00:50:52.570 --> 00:50:55.690
As the name suggests, they try
to model how human perception

00:50:55.690 --> 00:50:58.900
or how human distortion works.

00:50:58.900 --> 00:51:00.790
And you can roughly
think of them

00:51:00.790 --> 00:51:03.020
as part of being three classes.

00:51:03.020 --> 00:51:03.520
OK.

00:51:03.520 --> 00:51:04.978
Like there are
three ways you might

00:51:04.978 --> 00:51:08.380
want to attack this
problem as of today.

00:51:08.380 --> 00:51:11.410
So one way is like,
OK, one idea is maybe,

00:51:11.410 --> 00:51:13.990
we should model these low level
human vision features, right?

00:51:13.990 --> 00:51:16.390
We talked about all of
these different things.

00:51:16.390 --> 00:51:18.100
Why can't we model them, right?

00:51:18.100 --> 00:51:18.880
We can model it.

00:51:18.880 --> 00:51:21.525
And then instead of MSE, which
is a simple formula, maybe

00:51:21.525 --> 00:51:23.650
we'll have something a
little bit more complicated.

00:51:23.650 --> 00:51:25.810
And we'll use that as
a distortion metric.

00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:28.480
And that will be a better guide
of how to do rate distortion

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:31.060
optimization, OK?

00:51:31.060 --> 00:51:35.050
The second idea is to use
like this modern, big learning

00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:36.910
machine learning models, right?

00:51:36.910 --> 00:51:40.000
A lot of people have figured
out that, oh, these actually

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:42.610
extract very relevant
features, very similar features

00:51:42.610 --> 00:51:43.900
to humans.

00:51:43.900 --> 00:51:46.840
And maybe, we can use
like this huge models

00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:51.090
to actually extract the
relevant information out, OK?

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:54.060
So actually, let me
also throw some terms.

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:56.040
So this modeling
low-level human vision

00:51:56.040 --> 00:52:03.210
features led to metrics such
as SSIM, MS-SSIM or VIF.

00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:07.620
Again, if you go read any
paper on image compression,

00:52:07.620 --> 00:52:13.950
video compression, as of
today, they would almost 100%

00:52:13.950 --> 00:52:16.890
include some comparison
of distortion with SSIM.

00:52:16.890 --> 00:52:19.890
You probably can't even
publish or convince anyone

00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:21.330
that your compressor
works better

00:52:21.330 --> 00:52:24.390
if you only report results
for distortion with MSE.

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:26.370
So this is like a 2002 paper.

00:52:26.370 --> 00:52:28.590
It has really become
industry standard.

00:52:28.590 --> 00:52:30.340
Like not even industry academia.

00:52:30.340 --> 00:52:30.840
Everywhere.

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:31.680
It's a standard.

00:52:31.680 --> 00:52:33.690
That's like one of
the early works which

00:52:33.690 --> 00:52:37.150
tried to model human
visual perception.

00:52:37.150 --> 00:52:40.360
Moving on, this idea of
LPIPS, which is now using big

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:42.310
learned machine learning
models as a proxy

00:52:42.310 --> 00:52:45.550
to what human vision might
be doing and using them

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:47.500
as a measure of distortion.

00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:50.915
The most popular work is
something called LPIPS, right?

00:52:50.915 --> 00:52:52.540
You don't have to
remember these terms.

00:52:52.540 --> 00:52:54.082
You can come back
and see this slide,

00:52:54.082 --> 00:52:55.720
but you might see
these terms if you

00:52:55.720 --> 00:52:59.080
are working and doing research
in these areas very frequently.

00:52:59.080 --> 00:53:01.540
So that's another sort of--

00:53:01.540 --> 00:53:03.640
LPIPS itself is not
a distortion metric.

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:07.070
It's just a method
which using which you

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:09.560
exploit some distortion metric.

00:53:09.560 --> 00:53:12.980
And the basic idea is it
uses this learn models.

00:53:12.980 --> 00:53:14.660
And then the third
idea is somewhat

00:53:14.660 --> 00:53:18.050
in the middle, which
is OK, let me maybe

00:53:18.050 --> 00:53:19.850
come up with simple features.

00:53:19.850 --> 00:53:22.070
Not learned hand-design
features, right?

00:53:22.070 --> 00:53:24.380
Like somewhat like first one.

00:53:24.380 --> 00:53:28.940
But I'll combine them with
like different parameters

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:31.520
using machine learning,
like using some human vision

00:53:31.520 --> 00:53:33.040
experiments.

00:53:33.040 --> 00:53:35.560
So in this one, you are
learning all the features.

00:53:35.560 --> 00:53:36.910
In this one, it's everything.

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:38.950
Handwritten rules.

00:53:38.950 --> 00:53:41.530
The third one, it's
more like somewhere

00:53:41.530 --> 00:53:44.110
in the middle, where you know
what features to extract,

00:53:44.110 --> 00:53:46.630
but then you decide
how to combine them

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:49.250
by doing a lot of subjective
studies and so on.

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:53.050
So now, people did that, and
now it's fixed so as to say.

00:53:53.050 --> 00:53:59.590
And like the most famous
of this is like VMAF, which

00:53:59.590 --> 00:54:01.240
is used by Netflix popularized.

00:54:01.240 --> 00:54:03.460
And it's like any
video paper video,

00:54:03.460 --> 00:54:06.640
compression paper would have
a VMAF score related to it.

00:54:06.640 --> 00:54:08.000
It's very recent again.

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:10.360
So SSIM was something like 2002.

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:12.460
So all of these are
this century by the way.

00:54:12.460 --> 00:54:14.470
Before that, we
weren't even at level

00:54:14.470 --> 00:54:16.370
that we cared about things.

00:54:16.370 --> 00:54:18.130
So SSIM something like 2002.

00:54:18.130 --> 00:54:21.840
LPIPS I think is like 2018, '19.

00:54:21.840 --> 00:54:23.160
Something like that maybe. '20.

00:54:23.160 --> 00:54:25.740
And then VMAF is again,
I think 2015, '16.

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:26.530
Something of that.

00:54:26.530 --> 00:54:28.740
So all of these are
like pretty new methods,

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:31.140
and it's a really
hot area of research.

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:33.120
Like if you figure out
solve this problem,

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:36.180
it's really relevant
in terms of resources.

00:54:36.180 --> 00:54:38.220
You can save like
smartly allocate bits

00:54:38.220 --> 00:54:43.420
as well as like big money
for different aspects.

00:54:43.420 --> 00:54:43.920
OK.

00:54:43.920 --> 00:54:48.120
So I'm not going to go into too
much details about any of this,

00:54:48.120 --> 00:54:50.250
but I think all of
these are important.

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:52.350
So let me just give
you a 5-minute overview

00:54:52.350 --> 00:54:54.930
of the basic ideas used, OK?

00:54:54.930 --> 00:54:57.740
In these things.

00:54:57.740 --> 00:55:00.440
So SSIM.

00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:05.330
This is now a figure
from the paper.

00:55:05.330 --> 00:55:06.710
First look, you
might get scared.

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:07.418
Don't get scared.

00:55:07.418 --> 00:55:08.810
It's actually not that hard.

00:55:08.810 --> 00:55:11.660
The basic idea
really is like SSIM,

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:14.450
it's from Structural Similarity.

00:55:14.450 --> 00:55:17.360
That's where the
SSIM terms come from.

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:20.360
It uses three key features
to basically compare

00:55:20.360 --> 00:55:21.200
any two images.

00:55:21.200 --> 00:55:23.570
So distortion is between
two images, right?

00:55:23.570 --> 00:55:26.390
Like you need original
reconstructed or between two.

00:55:26.390 --> 00:55:28.590
It's like distance.

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:30.570
The way it constructs
that is it takes

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:33.420
into account the luminescence
because it says like, OK,

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:36.450
the average y-value is what
we as humans care about.

00:55:36.450 --> 00:55:39.300
The contrast, again,
something, we as humans

00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:41.340
care about and the structure.

00:55:41.340 --> 00:55:42.930
And this is the
paper actually, which

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:45.600
is the source of this image,
which we have been showing.

00:55:45.600 --> 00:55:47.490
Like they were the ones
to very clearly show

00:55:47.490 --> 00:55:49.680
that these things matter.

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:51.930
The way you compute
luminescence is just

00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:54.030
like mean of blocks x and y.

00:55:54.030 --> 00:55:57.990
And so you have some L between
x and y luminescence, distance

00:55:57.990 --> 00:56:01.230
between x and y images, which
is just some function of mean

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:02.310
of x and mean of y.

00:56:02.310 --> 00:56:04.410
Let's not get into
details, but again,

00:56:04.410 --> 00:56:06.600
any topic about this
lecture if it interests

00:56:06.600 --> 00:56:07.740
you, come talk to me later.

00:56:07.740 --> 00:56:09.360
We can discuss more.

00:56:09.360 --> 00:56:12.960
The contrast, like I said,
it's just like variance, right?

00:56:12.960 --> 00:56:14.760
much how many these
pixels are changing,

00:56:14.760 --> 00:56:16.200
and that's how they model it.

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:20.430
Again, like some function of
the sigma x and sigma y, OK?

00:56:20.430 --> 00:56:28.480
And the structural similarity
is just the idea that basically,

00:56:28.480 --> 00:56:31.390
what sort of the covariance
between these two images,

00:56:31.390 --> 00:56:32.320
right?

00:56:32.320 --> 00:56:34.240
Do they have the edges
at the same places?

00:56:34.240 --> 00:56:36.850
Do they have the shallow
regions at the same places,

00:56:36.850 --> 00:56:39.370
so on and so forth, right?

00:56:39.370 --> 00:56:42.700
And that's being captured by
basically the sigma xy, which

00:56:42.700 --> 00:56:45.550
is just the covariance
matrix or correlation

00:56:45.550 --> 00:56:49.340
matrix between these
two images x and y.

00:56:49.340 --> 00:56:50.900
And then you take this.

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:52.430
You compute these three scores.

00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:55.940
That's what's being shown in
a diagrammatic fashion here.

00:56:55.940 --> 00:56:57.650
And then you combine
these three scores

00:56:57.650 --> 00:57:01.610
to get this SSIM score finally
between two images x and y.

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:06.620
And it looks something
like this where

00:57:06.620 --> 00:57:08.840
you have different values
of alpha, beta, gamma.

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:10.760
It results in some formula.

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:13.940
Today, you will find like many,
many implementations for SSIM,

00:57:13.940 --> 00:57:16.220
but the idea is actually
not that hard, right?

00:57:16.220 --> 00:57:18.890
All you are doing is
computing some second order

00:57:18.890 --> 00:57:20.480
terms between these two images.

00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:24.110
And then you are using them
as distortion metric, OK?

00:57:24.110 --> 00:57:26.540
And again, all of this
[AUDIO OUT] visually motivated.

00:57:26.540 --> 00:57:27.950
Luminescence is important.

00:57:27.950 --> 00:57:29.010
Contrast is important.

00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:30.260
Structure is important, right?

00:57:30.260 --> 00:57:31.968
It's going away from
the mSE so it's just

00:57:31.968 --> 00:57:34.750
trying to model this
low level behaviors.

00:57:38.060 --> 00:57:38.600
OK.

00:57:38.600 --> 00:57:40.520
So I'm not going to go
into details of this

00:57:40.520 --> 00:57:42.860
but another very
interesting factor is

00:57:42.860 --> 00:57:48.170
the way you compute this mean
variance and the covariance.

00:57:48.170 --> 00:57:51.500
So that's typically
like for example mean.

00:57:51.500 --> 00:57:53.900
It's not just taking
average of pixels.

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:56.630
It's actually some
weighted average of pixels.

00:57:56.630 --> 00:57:59.390
And again, the only reason I
want to tell you about this

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:03.150
like why you want weighted
average of pixels--

00:58:03.150 --> 00:58:03.650
OK.

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:04.560
It's a heuristic.

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:07.680
There isn't really like
a theorem to prove it,

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:10.920
but the idea really is like
it of accounts for foveation,

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:13.800
which says that, oh, if
I'm focusing on this block

00:58:13.800 --> 00:58:16.020
and I'm calculating like
the difference in two

00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:18.780
images and one
particular block, I

00:58:18.780 --> 00:58:21.840
should put more value to the
center aspects than the side

00:58:21.840 --> 00:58:24.090
aspects because in some
sense, when I'm going to look,

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:26.790
I'm going to look less
towards the side, right?

00:58:26.790 --> 00:58:30.240
So this weight aspect is
sort of a Gaussian kernel.

00:58:30.240 --> 00:58:32.070
Motivation is foveation.

00:58:32.070 --> 00:58:36.840
Again, it's all hand-wavy, but
that's why they do it, right?

00:58:36.840 --> 00:58:41.520
And then what they could
show is that this SSIM metric

00:58:41.520 --> 00:58:43.680
collaborates much
better with humans

00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:46.080
judgment compared to
the mean square error.

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.210
So in future, people
also extended it

00:58:51.210 --> 00:58:53.010
to some more interesting ideas.

00:58:53.010 --> 00:58:54.540
There is something
called MS-SSIM.

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:55.660
Again, very common.

00:58:55.660 --> 00:58:57.390
MS-SSIM for now multiscale.

00:58:57.390 --> 00:59:01.320
And the idea really is you
keep applying this SSIM.

00:59:01.320 --> 00:59:04.500
So you apply SSIM for the
signal 1 and signal 2.

00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:06.240
Then you downsample
both of them.

00:59:06.240 --> 00:59:07.200
Then you apply again.

00:59:07.200 --> 00:59:08.695
Then you downsample
both of them.

00:59:08.695 --> 00:59:09.570
Then you apply again.

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:10.740
And you keep doing that.

00:59:10.740 --> 00:59:13.710
And then you somehow
combine them.

00:59:13.710 --> 00:59:15.400
Why would you want to do that?

00:59:15.400 --> 00:59:18.660
So there, the idea
is you want to see

00:59:18.660 --> 00:59:21.210
how this image responds to
various different spatial

00:59:21.210 --> 00:59:22.680
frequencies really.

00:59:22.680 --> 00:59:25.020
And the idea is
that we almost have

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.750
different independent channels
for different spatial frequency

00:59:27.750 --> 00:59:30.300
or contrast behaves
differently, so on and so forth.

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:31.770
And what they
showed us like this

00:59:31.770 --> 00:59:34.410
is like a decent
improvement over SSIM.

00:59:34.410 --> 00:59:37.740
So again, like a very
commonly used metric

00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:40.815
in all sorts of image and
video compression works.

00:59:44.130 --> 00:59:44.630
All right.

00:59:44.630 --> 00:59:49.730
Then the second idea
was around this LPIPS.

00:59:49.730 --> 00:59:53.630
And this is just like a
quote from this paper.

00:59:53.630 --> 00:59:57.950
LPIPS is again, so a lot
of acronyms in this area.

00:59:57.950 --> 01:00:00.620
I don't know why,
but I think it's OK.

01:00:00.620 --> 01:00:05.030
So LPIPS is for Learned
Perceptual Image Patch

01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:06.410
Similarity.

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:08.622
And I think the name kind of--

01:00:08.622 --> 01:00:10.580
like if you just want a
thousand feet overview,

01:00:10.580 --> 01:00:14.930
the name gives you
what it might be doing.

01:00:14.930 --> 01:00:16.820
The basic idea
really is-- and this

01:00:16.820 --> 01:00:19.487
is like an image
from the same paper.

01:00:19.487 --> 01:00:21.320
So this is like a quote
from the paper which

01:00:21.320 --> 01:00:25.160
says, "Our results suggest
that perceptual similarity is

01:00:25.160 --> 01:00:27.860
an emergent property
shared across deep visual

01:00:27.860 --> 01:00:29.600
representations."

01:00:29.600 --> 01:00:31.610
Very fancy word.

01:00:31.610 --> 01:00:32.480
Very fancy line.

01:00:32.480 --> 01:00:35.930
But if you understand machine
learning, what these are saying

01:00:35.930 --> 01:00:38.960
is that you learn
features themselves

01:00:38.960 --> 01:00:43.850
are somewhat representative of
what humans would have looked

01:00:43.850 --> 01:00:45.950
as features in these studies.

01:00:45.950 --> 01:00:49.280
And the way they go
about doing it is they

01:00:49.280 --> 01:00:52.370
use this deep embeddings
as a feature space.

01:00:52.370 --> 01:00:56.300
And then define some distances
over these embeddings,

01:00:56.300 --> 01:00:58.400
neural net embeddings,
which are like outputs

01:00:58.400 --> 01:01:01.610
of these neural
nets to determine,

01:01:01.610 --> 01:01:06.700
OK, a new distortion
metric for humans.

01:01:06.700 --> 01:01:07.200
Oh, sorry.

01:01:07.200 --> 01:01:10.850
A new distortion
metric for compressors.

01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:13.490
And so like for example, what
you can see here is let's say,

01:01:13.490 --> 01:01:16.460
this is your reference image.

01:01:16.460 --> 01:01:18.740
This is like one
compressed image,

01:01:18.740 --> 01:01:20.630
and this is like another
compressed version

01:01:20.630 --> 01:01:21.650
of that image.

01:01:21.650 --> 01:01:23.360
Humans would choose
this right one.

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:26.870
But if you were
doing L2, so this one

01:01:26.870 --> 01:01:29.720
has a lower mean squared
error compared to these two.

01:01:29.720 --> 01:01:31.880
Clearly, but the right
one is much better, right?

01:01:31.880 --> 01:01:33.525
So, this is again
another illustration

01:01:33.525 --> 01:01:34.400
of what we have seen.

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:37.100
But more interestingly,
when SSIM, which we just

01:01:37.100 --> 01:01:41.180
talked about would
choose this one,

01:01:41.180 --> 01:01:43.640
whereas their method,
like whatever these three

01:01:43.640 --> 01:01:45.080
different networks--
think of this

01:01:45.080 --> 01:01:46.580
as some learned representation.

01:01:46.580 --> 01:01:49.550
Some distance metric over
these deep embeddings--

01:01:49.550 --> 01:01:51.650
would have chosen this patch.

01:01:51.650 --> 01:01:54.410
So it shows that
these neural nets,

01:01:54.410 --> 01:01:55.910
the embeddings
learned through them.

01:01:55.910 --> 01:02:00.440
May be doing something
more similar to humans.

01:02:00.440 --> 01:02:02.420
Yeah, we're not going
to go through this.

01:02:02.420 --> 01:02:05.150
The important really
is this line here

01:02:05.150 --> 01:02:07.880
that what you can do
is for each image,

01:02:07.880 --> 01:02:10.400
you can extract its
embeddings, which

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:13.430
is just the output of this
[AUDIO OUT] neural nets.

01:02:13.430 --> 01:02:15.560
And then you can
define some distance

01:02:15.560 --> 01:02:18.470
between these
embeddings as a proxy

01:02:18.470 --> 01:02:20.720
to whatever your distortion
measure between these two

01:02:20.720 --> 01:02:21.440
images.

01:02:21.440 --> 01:02:25.310
And again, it's now very
commonly [AUDIO OUT]

01:02:25.310 --> 01:02:27.740
reported [AUDIO OUT] first.

01:02:27.740 --> 01:02:28.790
I'm going to skip this.

01:02:31.050 --> 01:02:31.550
Oh.

01:02:31.550 --> 01:02:33.950
Any [AUDIO OUT].

01:02:33.950 --> 01:02:34.500
Nope?

01:02:34.500 --> 01:02:35.000
OK.

01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.280
And so the third
idea is this VMAF.

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:40.280
I'm actually not going to
go into details of this,

01:02:40.280 --> 01:02:42.320
but again, like the
basic idea here really

01:02:42.320 --> 01:02:45.020
is you have some
predefined features.

01:02:45.020 --> 01:02:47.330
So you have some predefined
spatial features.

01:02:47.330 --> 01:02:51.800
Think of this VIF, DLM
as some matrix like SSIM.

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:54.200
Like we can talk about
it, but think of them

01:02:54.200 --> 01:02:55.460
as like brothers of SSIM.

01:02:55.460 --> 01:02:58.160
Like people did more
work in the similar class

01:02:58.160 --> 01:03:00.500
of defining low level vision.

01:03:00.500 --> 01:03:02.660
And then you can
extract all of these.

01:03:02.660 --> 01:03:06.020
But then finally, like how you
combine all this four or five

01:03:06.020 --> 01:03:08.000
different existing--

01:03:08.000 --> 01:03:09.140
actually exactly two.

01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:11.840
But how you combine
these metrics, which

01:03:11.840 --> 01:03:14.060
exist at low level
humans is now you

01:03:14.060 --> 01:03:16.250
train a machine learning
model to figure out

01:03:16.250 --> 01:03:20.370
how to combine these metrics by
collecting a lot of human data.

01:03:20.370 --> 01:03:22.710
So you ask a lot of people,
OK, which one do you prefer?

01:03:22.710 --> 01:03:23.550
Which one do you prefer?

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:24.690
Which one do you prefer?

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:27.750
And then you try to figure out,
OK, what's the right distance?

01:03:27.750 --> 01:03:30.630
And that's like another
idea of coming up

01:03:30.630 --> 01:03:32.670
with a perceptual metric, right?

01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:34.860
And so these trained
parameters are fixed,

01:03:34.860 --> 01:03:37.995
and then you can use that as
another distortion metric.

01:03:42.170 --> 01:03:44.720
Going back, so these are
roughly like how I tried

01:03:44.720 --> 01:03:46.880
to summarize this sort of area.

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:50.330
So there are roughly three kind
of three classes of things,

01:03:50.330 --> 01:03:54.170
which people are doing to come
up with new distortion metrics.

01:03:54.170 --> 01:03:56.370
Very, very hot area of research.

01:03:56.370 --> 01:03:59.450
So like I think it's
far from mature.

01:03:59.450 --> 01:04:01.700
So if you want to
contribute, it's

01:04:01.700 --> 01:04:08.280
like you can make big
impact by contributing.

01:04:08.280 --> 01:04:08.780
OK.

01:04:08.780 --> 01:04:10.430
Any questions?

01:04:10.430 --> 01:04:12.860
Then we'll just do
the last part of where

01:04:12.860 --> 01:04:16.330
human vision can play a role.

01:04:16.330 --> 01:04:16.830
OK.

01:04:16.830 --> 01:04:18.538
So, so far, we have
looked at two things.

01:04:18.538 --> 01:04:21.450
Traditional compressors like
how a lot of design decisions.

01:04:21.450 --> 01:04:23.670
The second one
was, oh, MSE is bad

01:04:23.670 --> 01:04:25.920
so let's figure out like
what are the right distortion

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:28.410
measures, which are
perceptually relevant.

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:31.360
Now, the third thing is
actually even more interesting.

01:04:31.360 --> 01:04:34.980
So we have talked about
rate distortion trade-off.

01:04:34.980 --> 01:04:38.610
But again, a very recent work--
this is I think 2018, '19.

01:04:38.610 --> 01:04:41.910
So all of these are very new.

01:04:41.910 --> 01:04:45.120
Talks about rate distortion
and perception trade-off.

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:51.000
And here is like a screenshot
from this paper, which I think

01:04:51.000 --> 01:04:52.317
is very illustrative.

01:04:52.317 --> 01:04:54.150
So let's say you have
an image [AUDIO OUT]..

01:04:54.150 --> 01:04:56.130
So like [AUDIO OUT].

01:04:56.130 --> 01:04:57.960
You are sitting
outside at Stanford.

01:04:57.960 --> 01:04:59.040
You saw some grass.

01:04:59.040 --> 01:05:00.900
You click a picture, OK?

01:05:00.900 --> 01:05:08.030
This is what that image looks
like compressed using JPEG, OK?

01:05:08.030 --> 01:05:13.120
And the third one is
think of it as our deep--

01:05:13.120 --> 01:05:14.230
it's a fake image.

01:05:14.230 --> 01:05:17.860
It's a neural net generated
image of that grass, which

01:05:17.860 --> 01:05:19.750
says statistically
represent grass,

01:05:19.750 --> 01:05:21.820
but it's not exactly
the same grass.

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:23.830
How do I know?

01:05:23.830 --> 01:05:26.020
Maybe, look at this strand here.

01:05:26.020 --> 01:05:27.520
You see there is a strand here.

01:05:27.520 --> 01:05:28.720
There is this strand here.

01:05:28.720 --> 01:05:30.520
There is no strand here, right?

01:05:30.520 --> 01:05:32.110
So it's just a general grass.

01:05:32.110 --> 01:05:34.960
Generated grass, OK?

01:05:34.960 --> 01:05:39.040
But if I ask you,
like amongst B and C,

01:05:39.040 --> 01:05:41.740
which reconstruction
do you prefer?

01:05:41.740 --> 01:05:45.790
How many of you say B?

01:05:45.790 --> 01:05:47.260
How many of you would say C?

01:05:50.930 --> 01:05:52.955
B or C?

01:05:52.955 --> 01:05:54.260
[INAUDIBLE]

01:05:54.260 --> 01:05:55.190
Oh.

01:05:55.190 --> 01:05:57.365
B compared with A or
C compared with A.

01:05:57.365 --> 01:05:58.490
Which one would you prefer?

01:05:58.490 --> 01:06:00.140
Let's say A was
your source image.

01:06:00.140 --> 01:06:06.340
One compressor compresses at
2 B. The other [AUDIO OUT] C.

01:06:06.340 --> 01:06:07.070
C.

01:06:07.070 --> 01:06:07.900
OK.

01:06:07.900 --> 01:06:09.310
Maybe, that's not a bad.

01:06:09.310 --> 01:06:14.800
Yeah, I think I would prefer C.
But because B is like it's just

01:06:14.800 --> 01:06:18.300
blurred even though it has
the right strands everywhere.

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:21.720
But clearly, C has a very high
distortion in terms of MSE.

01:06:21.720 --> 01:06:24.740
It's just like random
arrangement of strands.

01:06:24.740 --> 01:06:27.230
But we, as humans, don't really
care about these strands,

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:27.730
right?

01:06:27.730 --> 01:06:30.320
If this was somebody's face,
we would be like mad, right?

01:06:30.320 --> 01:06:32.390
Like if somebody's face
was distorted like this,

01:06:32.390 --> 01:06:34.950
we'll just lose our stuff.

01:06:34.950 --> 01:06:39.410
But like for this
grass or in general,

01:06:39.410 --> 01:06:42.770
for a lot of texture kind
of images and details--

01:06:42.770 --> 01:06:43.940
for example, this carpet.

01:06:43.940 --> 01:06:46.400
I don't really care where
exactly this is as long

01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:49.340
as statistically they
are somewhat similar,

01:06:49.340 --> 01:06:52.740
or in other words, perceptually,
they look the same.

01:06:52.740 --> 01:06:56.720
And that's really like sort of
a very new and upcoming area

01:06:56.720 --> 01:06:59.210
both in terms of
theory and practice,

01:06:59.210 --> 01:07:01.490
and lots of open and
interesting questions

01:07:01.490 --> 01:07:04.910
there which is, how do you
account for this perception

01:07:04.910 --> 01:07:06.650
when you are doing compression?

01:07:06.650 --> 01:07:09.410
Essentially, it's not just
about rate distortion.

01:07:09.410 --> 01:07:14.140
Maybe, I should think about
the perception aspect too.

01:07:14.140 --> 01:07:16.990
And so this is like another
example from this paper.

01:07:16.990 --> 01:07:19.550
So what they do is like, OK,
you have some rate definition.

01:07:19.550 --> 01:07:20.800
Don't worry about the symbols.

01:07:20.800 --> 01:07:23.080
Your rate is some
function of X, X hat.

01:07:23.080 --> 01:07:25.840
You have some distortion
which is expected value of we

01:07:25.840 --> 01:07:29.260
have been looking at d as
a symbol for distortion.

01:07:29.260 --> 01:07:32.620
Now, you also model perception.

01:07:32.620 --> 01:07:35.050
And the way you model
perception is some distance

01:07:35.050 --> 01:07:37.700
between the probability
distributions of your original

01:07:37.700 --> 01:07:39.700
and reconstructed because
at the end of the day,

01:07:39.700 --> 01:07:44.290
your perception is like some
statistical, some measurement

01:07:44.290 --> 01:07:46.240
on the probability distribution.

01:07:46.240 --> 01:07:49.060
And now, instead of doing
just R plus lambda d,

01:07:49.060 --> 01:07:51.770
you would try to basically
play with these 3 parameters.

01:07:51.770 --> 01:07:57.490
So not just trade off 2 but
maybe play with 3, right?

01:07:57.490 --> 01:08:00.880
So here is basically an
example of what happens.

01:08:00.880 --> 01:08:05.190
Let's just look at
this last row, OK?

01:08:05.190 --> 01:08:05.690
Sorry.

01:08:05.690 --> 01:08:06.590
Yeah, last row.

01:08:06.590 --> 01:08:09.140
So this is the input.

01:08:09.140 --> 01:08:11.090
So this is our MNIST
digits, which we also

01:08:11.090 --> 01:08:12.810
saw in the last class.

01:08:12.810 --> 01:08:15.650
So same MNIST digits.

01:08:15.650 --> 01:08:20.600
And now, I'm trying to compress
it to 2 bits and then decode.

01:08:20.600 --> 01:08:21.170
Sorry.

01:08:21.170 --> 01:08:22.729
Compress it to 2
bits and then decode

01:08:22.729 --> 01:08:25.310
using different trade-offs
between the distortion

01:08:25.310 --> 01:08:26.000
and perception.

01:08:26.000 --> 01:08:27.740
So I fixed the rate.

01:08:27.740 --> 01:08:31.040
I can change my distortion
and perception, right?

01:08:31.040 --> 01:08:33.229
So the first column
here is what happens

01:08:33.229 --> 01:08:36.229
if you were to optimally
compress it using Shannon.

01:08:36.229 --> 01:08:37.609
So just rate distortion.

01:08:37.609 --> 01:08:39.859
You don't care about
the perception.

01:08:39.859 --> 01:08:41.660
And as you move
towards the right,

01:08:41.660 --> 01:08:43.910
you give higher and higher
penalty for perception.

01:08:43.910 --> 01:08:46.250
So you care more and
more about perception.

01:08:46.250 --> 01:08:48.590
What do you see that at 2 bits?

01:08:48.590 --> 01:08:50.300
Like this is very blurred.

01:08:50.300 --> 01:08:51.830
Like this is like--

01:08:51.830 --> 01:08:52.490
right?

01:08:52.490 --> 01:08:55.850
Versus as you keep
going towards right,

01:08:55.850 --> 01:08:58.819
it's become higher
and higher fidelity.

01:08:58.819 --> 01:09:02.180
But now, what might happen is
like your numbers might not

01:09:02.180 --> 01:09:04.890
match exactly because
again, the same thing is

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:07.200
like here, the strands
didn't match exactly.

01:09:07.200 --> 01:09:09.149
Here, the numbers
won't match exactly.

01:09:09.149 --> 01:09:11.939
So there is some trade off
so as to say which you now

01:09:11.939 --> 01:09:14.819
need to figure out between
what exact distortion

01:09:14.819 --> 01:09:16.470
and perceptions
you can work with.

01:09:16.470 --> 01:09:18.930
And obviously, I
think something which

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:20.939
is very important
to understanding

01:09:20.939 --> 01:09:23.939
this is if you had infinite
rate, if you are working

01:09:23.939 --> 01:09:25.800
in a high bandwidth,
high streaming

01:09:25.800 --> 01:09:28.380
kind of an application, sure.

01:09:28.380 --> 01:09:30.689
You would like to represent
everything exactly.

01:09:30.689 --> 01:09:34.290
But the more and more
advantages of this RDP or taking

01:09:34.290 --> 01:09:37.260
perception into account starts
coming as you are really

01:09:37.260 --> 01:09:38.310
rate limited, right?

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:40.740
Because then, you can't
spend a lot of bits

01:09:40.740 --> 01:09:44.970
to represent each of that
MSE like perfectly, right?

01:09:44.970 --> 01:09:47.910
At that point, it's not about
seeing the most pristine, most

01:09:47.910 --> 01:09:52.229
like all the edges, the perfect
image, and your HDTV, right?

01:09:52.229 --> 01:09:54.270
At that point, you care
more about something,

01:09:54.270 --> 01:09:57.150
which is more acceptable
to me than like maybe

01:09:57.150 --> 01:09:58.080
these blurred symbols.

01:09:58.080 --> 01:10:00.413
Like these blurred symbols
is something like as a human,

01:10:00.413 --> 01:10:01.230
I won't accept.

01:10:01.230 --> 01:10:03.330
This is extremely bad quality.

01:10:03.330 --> 01:10:06.330
I would rather expect
maybe slight distortions.

01:10:06.330 --> 01:10:10.920
Maybe not at this extreme
where my 5 became 7, right?

01:10:10.920 --> 01:10:16.980
So this is just bad, but maybe,
at 5 bits, being 5 is nice,

01:10:16.980 --> 01:10:17.480
right?

01:10:17.480 --> 01:10:20.690
So something to be basically--

01:10:20.690 --> 01:10:23.510
it's very up and coming and
something to think about.

01:10:23.510 --> 01:10:24.590
And really maybe, right?

01:10:24.590 --> 01:10:29.080
Like it's really
interesting and important.

01:10:29.080 --> 01:10:30.640
And so all of this is nice.

01:10:30.640 --> 01:10:32.770
So this is like sort of
some theory component,

01:10:32.770 --> 01:10:34.660
and there are some
very nice new theorems.

01:10:34.660 --> 01:10:38.260
So if you guys like the
theory part of RD, like again,

01:10:38.260 --> 01:10:39.520
these are not old papers.

01:10:39.520 --> 01:10:43.570
These are like 2019 I think
if I recall correctly.

01:10:43.570 --> 01:10:47.530
And so like more
recently, even otherwise,

01:10:47.530 --> 01:10:51.460
like so this paper talks about
that you can bound the maximum.

01:10:51.460 --> 01:10:54.010
So ideally, you would
like to bound some error

01:10:54.010 --> 01:10:55.690
on the distortion, right?

01:10:55.690 --> 01:10:57.010
Like you wouldn't want--

01:10:57.010 --> 01:10:59.230
like OK, if I go for
perception, I should completely

01:10:59.230 --> 01:10:59.980
change the number.

01:10:59.980 --> 01:11:03.220
If I do that, then that's a
very bad, bad thing, right?

01:11:03.220 --> 01:11:06.910
I can't really work
with that formulation.

01:11:06.910 --> 01:11:09.250
So for example, in theory
what this paper showed

01:11:09.250 --> 01:11:12.310
is that, oh, if you were
under certain starma,

01:11:12.310 --> 01:11:16.660
certain conditions, you can
bound the error in distortion,

01:11:16.660 --> 01:11:20.390
even if you were optimally
optimizing for perception.

01:11:20.390 --> 01:11:22.310
So that's a good
result. That then starts

01:11:22.310 --> 01:11:24.680
saying that, OK, if
I do this smartly,

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:29.500
maybe it won't be the case
ever that my 5 becomes 1.

01:11:29.500 --> 01:11:32.537
I'm being handwavy, but
that's really the idea.

01:11:32.537 --> 01:11:34.370
And there is like a lot
of open field there.

01:11:34.370 --> 01:11:35.930
People haven't
really explored this

01:11:35.930 --> 01:11:38.850
as much whereas RD has been
explored for the past 50 years,

01:11:38.850 --> 01:11:39.350
right?

01:11:39.350 --> 01:11:40.027
50, 60 years.

01:11:40.027 --> 01:11:42.110
So again, if you're
interested in theory something

01:11:42.110 --> 01:11:44.180
nice to look forward to.

01:11:44.180 --> 01:11:48.230
But in practice also
like this RDP framework

01:11:48.230 --> 01:11:51.080
is already being used in
many different senses.

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:55.340
So recall this picture from
a [AUDIO OUT] lecture, right?

01:11:55.340 --> 01:11:57.950
We had this particular
image, and then we

01:11:57.950 --> 01:12:01.020
were trying to
optimize [AUDIO OUT]..

